      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}-24x+{y}^{2}+6y=-137.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-24x+{y}^{2}+6y & =-137 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-24x)+({y}^{2}+6y) & =-137 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 12x+{12}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =-137+{12}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 12x+{12}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-12)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-12)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(12,-3)$ and its radius is $4.$",
