      "problem": "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3x-y-2=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3x-y-2 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+3x)+({y}^{2}-y) & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =2+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {(x-(-\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}.$",
